 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "The sun gives out radiation of many kinds: visible light, infrared (heat), ultraviolet (UV), and also other rays. Sunlight takes about 8 minutes (about 500 seconds) to travel the 150 million kilometres to the Earth, at about 300 000 km/s.\nUses: light for seeing and for photosynthesis, heat, solar energy, vitamin D made in the skin.\nDangers: too much UV causes sunburn, eye damage and skin cancer. The ozone layer high in the atmosphere absorbs most UV.\nProtection: avoid the strongest sun around midday, wear a hat, cover the skin, never look straight at the sun.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "d": 150000000
  },
  "problem": "Light travels at about {300000} km/s. The sun is {d} km away. How long does sunlight take to reach the Earth?",
  "steps": [
   "Time = distance ÷ speed.",
   "{d} ÷ {300000} = {lightTime(d)} s (about eight minutes)."
  ],
  "answer": "{lightTime(d)} s",
  "checks": []
 },
